(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-07-risk-from-disease-to-exposure-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 7: Risk: From Disease to Exposure Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a case-control study of oral contraceptives and myocardial infarction (heart attack), exposure to birth control pills was abstracted from medical records at the time of the myocardial infarction. Results might be biased toward finding an association by all of the following except:","choices":["Physicians might have asked about use of birth control pill more carefully in cases.","Having a myocardial infarction might have led to oral contraceptive use.","Physicians might have been more likely to record birth control use in cases.","Medical record abstractors might have looked for evidence of oral contraceptive use more carefully if they knew a patient had had a myocardial infarction.","Patients might have recalled exposure more readily when they had a heart attack."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the exception. Pill use abstracted from records at the time of the infarction reflects use that came before the event, so the infarction cannot have led to it and creates no false excess of exposure in cases. The other statements each describe exposure being asked about, recorded, abstracted, or recalled more readily in cases, which falsely raises the odds ratio. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.1"},{"q":"Investigators in Europe did a case-control study, nested in a multicountry cohort of more than 520,000 participants, of vitamin D concentration and the risk of colon cancer. They studied 1,248 cases of incident colon cancer arising in the cohort and an equal number of controls, sampled from the same cohort and matched by age, sex, and study center. Vitamin D was measured in blood samples taken years before diagnosis. Vitamin D levels were lower in patients with colon cancer, independent of a rich array of potentially confounding variables. The study results could be described by any of the following except:","choices":["Vitamin D deficiency was a cause of colorectal cancer.","Vitamin D levels were associated with colorectal cancer.","Vitamin D deficiency was a risk factor for colorectal cancer.","Nesting the study in a large cohort was a strength of the study.","The results might have been confounded with unmeasured variables related to vitamin D levels and colorectal cancer."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A case-control study, even a well-designed one nested in a cohort, cannot claim to have identified a cause: unmeasured confounding is always possible, and risk factors may predict disease without causing it (they may be disease markers). — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is the most direct result of a case-control study?","choices":["Prevalence","Odds ratio","Risk difference","Relative risk","Incidence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The odds ratio (relative odds) is the measure of association for case-control studies, calculated as the odds of exposure in cases divided by the odds of exposure in controls, which equals ad over bc; it serves as an estimated relative risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 40; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.3"},{"q":"The epidemic curve for an acute infectious disease describes:","choices":["The usual incubation period for the causal agent","A comparison of illness over time in exposed versus nonexposed people","The onset of illness in cases over time","The duration of illness, on average, in affected individuals","The distribution of time from infection to first symptoms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An epidemic (epi) curve is the number of cases plotted against time, usually by date of onset of illness, so it shows the rise and fall of cases over the course of the outbreak, as in the Legionnaires' disease outbreak plotted by date of onset. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best reason for doing a case-control analysis of a cohort study?","choices":["Case-control studies are a feasible way of controlling for confounders not found in the cohort dataset.","Case-control studies can provide all the same information more easily.","Case-control studies can determine incidence of disease in exposed and nonexposed members of the cohort.","Case-control studies are in general stronger than cohort studies."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If the cohort data lack variables that should be controlled for, a case-control analysis within the cohort is more efficient because the extra data need to be collected only for the cases and a sample of controls, not for the entire cohort. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.5"},{"q":"The best way to identify cases is to obtain them from:","choices":["A sample from the general (dynamic) population","Primary care physicians' offices","A community","A cohort representative of the population","A hospital"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sampling cases (and controls) from a cohort representative of the population is best, especially when exposure or disease changes rapidly over time or when controls are not matched to the date of disease onset in cases. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.6"},{"q":"What is the best reason to include multiple control groups in a case-control study?","choices":["To obtain a stronger estimate of relative risk","There are a limited number of cases and an ample number of potential controls","To control for confounding","To increase the generalizability of the result","The main control group may be systematically different from cases (other than on the exposure of interest)"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The main control group may differ from the cases in ways other than the exposure of interest; using a second control group shows whether results change substantially, and if they do the findings are called into question. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.7"},{"q":"A case-control study was conducted based on a registry of 10,000 children that were genetically predisposed to develop celiac disease (characterized by sensitivity to gluten in the diet that can lead to malabsorption). Investigators wanted to see if enterovirus infection was associated with increased risk of developing the disease in these children. The children were followed with regular blood and stool tests for 10 years. After 10 years, a sample of 100 cases was selected from 2,000 children who developed the disease, and 20% of these children had a preceding enterovirus infection. 200 Controls were selected from the remaining children without the disease, and 13% of these children had enterovirus diagnosed. Which of the following is true?","choices":["They crude odds ratio provides a good approximation of crude relative risk.","The sampling method for controls is inappropriate because only noncases are studied.","The crude odds ratio will overestimate relative risk.","The control selection will provide a direct estimate of population exposure."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Controls were chosen from children still disease-free after the study ended, and the disease is common (20%), so the odds ratio overestimates relative risk when it is above 1. Here the crude odds ratio is about 1.67, while a relative risk worked out for the whole registry (assuming the sampled exposure percentages hold for all cases and noncases) is about 1.5. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.8"},{"q":"Case-control studies can be used to study all of the following except:","choices":["The early symptoms of stomach cancer","Risk factors for sudden infant death syndrome","The incidence of suicide in the adult population","The protective effect of aspirin","Modes of transmission of an infectious disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Incidence is the number of new cases over time in a population of known size; case-control studies choose their cases and controls, so they cannot provide incidence (a cohort study establishes incidence directly). — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.9"},{"q":"In a case-control study of exercise and sudden cardiac death, matching would be useful:","choices":["To control for all potential confounding variables in the study","To make cases and controls similar to each other with respect to a few major characteristics","To make it possible to examine the effects of the matched variables on estimated relative risk","To test whether the right controls were chosen for the cases in the study","To increase the generalizability of the study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Matching selects controls with the same characteristics as cases on a few major variables (for example age and sex) except the exposure under study, so cases and controls do not differ on those variables that might be strongly related to exposure or disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.10"},{"q":"In a case-control study of whether prolonged air travel is a risk factor for venous thromboembolism, 60 out of 100 cases and 40 out of 100 controls had prolonged air travel. What was the crude odds ratio from this study?","choices":["0.44","1.5","2.25","3.0","Not possible to calculate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Odds of air travel in cases = 60/40 = 1.5; odds in controls = 40/60 = 0.67; odds ratio = 1.5 / 0.67 = 2.25, the same as the cross products (60 x 60) / (40 x 40) = 3,600 / 1,600. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.11"},{"q":"A population-based case-control study would be especially useful for studying:","choices":["The population attributable risk of disease","Multiple outcomes (diseases)","The incidence of rare diseases","The prevalence of disease","Risk factors for disease"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A case-control study compares people with and without one disease for earlier exposure, so it is useful for identifying risk factors for that disease, giving an odds ratio that estimates relative risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.12"},{"q":"The prevalence odds ratio of rheumatoid arthritis provides an estimate of:","choices":["The relative risk of arthritis","The attributable risk of arthritis","Risk factors for the duration of arthritis","The association between a patient characteristic and prevalence of arthritis","Risk factors for the incidence of arthritis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An odds ratio based on prevalent cases gives a rough measure of the association between a patient characteristic and having arthritis at that point in time, but not a comparison of risk, which requires incident (new-onset) cases. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.13"},{"q":"A case-control study was conducted to determine if children with asthma who used inhaled corticosteroids are at increased risk of fractures. A national registry was used to select cases and population-based controls, and possible confounders assessed by phone interview and chart review. Which of the following is incorrect?","choices":["Matching on sex would remove confounding for this factor","If there is no difference between the crude and adjusted odds ratio, then confounding for the measured factors was not present","Incidence of fracture in children using corticosteroids cannot be estimated","This study can identify risk factors but not establish cause","Difference in rates of participation in recreational sports between cases and controls would show that control selection was biased"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. This is the incorrect statement. A difference in sports participation between cases and controls may be a real association (sports may raise fracture risk) or a confounder, and does not prove that control selection was biased. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.14"},{"q":"In an outbreak of acute gastroenteritis, a case-control study would be especially useful for identifying:","choices":["Characteristics of the people affected","The number of people affected over time","The microbe or toxin causing the outbreak","The mode of transmission","Where the causative agent originated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Comparing the exposures of ill and well people tests hypotheses about how the disease is being spread (mode of transmission). That is the most pressing question, and answering it allows the outbreak to be controlled and its source traced. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 38; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39; 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 41; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.15"},{"q":"Sampling cases and controls from a defined population or cohort accomplishes which of the following?","choices":["It tends to include cases and controls that are representative of the source population.","It is the only way of including incident (new) cases of disease.","It avoids the need for inclusion and exclusion criteria.","It matches cases and controls on important variables.","It ensures that the results are generalizable."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Taking all or a random sample of cases and a random sample of controls from a defined population or cohort gives cases and controls that resemble those in the source population, reducing selection bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30; 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.16"},{"q":"Case-control studies would be useful for answering all of the following questions except:","choices":["Do cholesterol-lowering drugs prevent coronary heart disease?","Are complications more common with fiberoptic cholecystectomy than with conventional (open) surgery?","Is drinking alcohol a risk factor for breast cancer?","How often do complications occur after fiberoptic cholecystectomy?","How effective are antibiotics for otitis media?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. How often complications occur is an incidence question, and case-control studies do not provide incidence because the numbers of cases and controls are selected by the investigator; a cohort study is needed. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.17"},{"q":"In a case-control study of airplane flight and thrombophlebitis, all of the following conditions should be met for the odds ratio to be a reasonable estimate of relative risk except:","choices":["Controls were sampled from the same population as cases.","Cases and controls met the same inclusion and exclusion criteria.","Other variables that might be related to air travel and thrombosis were controlled for.","Cases and controls were equally susceptible to developing thrombophlebitis (e.g., were equally mobile, weight, recent trauma, previous VTE) other than for air travel.","The incidence of thrombophlebitis was more than 5/100."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. This is the exception: the odds ratio approximates relative risk when the disease is rare (a rule of thumb is under about 1 in 100 to 5 in 100), so high incidence is not a requirement. When the disease is more common and controls are sampled from noncases after the study, the odds ratio overestimates relative risk when it is above 1. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 7.18"}]};
